# Suppresses a known ThreadSanitizer limitation around the standard, correct
# fork()-safety idiom this module uses for its own slot-table rwlock (see
# _clog_atfork_release()'s own doc comment in src/clogger.c): a forked
# child's sole surviving thread re-initializes clog_slot_table.rwlock via
# pthread_rwlock_init() instead of unlocking it, since glibc's rwlock
# write-lock tracks ownership by TID and the child's post-fork thread has a
# different TID than the parent's forking thread did (a plain unlock would
# silently fail to release it). This is the same fix glibc's own malloc
# arena locks use for the identical problem, and is safe: the child has
# exactly one thread, so a fresh init can never race a second party.
#
# ThreadSanitizer does not clear its own internal "this rwlock is
# write-locked" shadow state across fork() + pthread_rwlock_init(): confirmed
# via an independent, clogger.c-free reproduction (a single global
# PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER-statically-initialized rwlock, write-locked in
# the parent, fork()ed, then re-initialized and read-locked in the child;
# no c_collections code involved at all) that reproduces the identical
# "read lock of a write locked mutex" report. The underlying rwlock is
# genuinely safe post-fork (POSIX explicitly permits it, and a plain unlock
# here, not a reinit, was independently verified via gdb to be what actually
# hangs); this is a detector limitation around the reinit-in-child pattern
# specifically, not a real race in this module's own logic.
#
# Anchored on _clog_resolve (this module's own rdlock call site, always
# symbol-resolved) rather than any pthread-internal frame name, mirroring
# tests/chttpclient/tsan_openssl.supp's own "anchor on our own code" choice.
mutex:_clog_resolve
